    "id": "carib-trinidad-tobago-sorrel-drink",
    "country": "Trinidad & Tobago",
    "name": "Caribbean Sorrel Drink (Trinidad Christmas Sorrel)",
    "source": "Cooking with Ria",
    "sourceUrl": "https://cookingwithria.com/2012/12/sorrel-drink/",
    "author": "CookingwithRia",
    "ingredients": [
      "5 oz sorrel, about 3 cups*** (1 ½ lbs fresh sorrel, uncleaned)",
      "12 cups water, 3 quarts",
      "2 cinnamon sticks",
      "20-25 cloves",
      "Ginger, optional",
      "2 cups sugar, white or raw brown, or to taste",
      "Rum or vodka, optional, to taste"
    ],
    "description": "Mummy's classic Trinidad sorrel drink. Dried sorrel is steeped overnight with cinnamon and cloves, then sweetened and served cold over ice.",
    "instructions": [
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Boil the spiced water: Add the water, cinnamon sticks, cloves and ginger (if using) to a large stockpot. Bring to a rolling boil over medium-high heat, about 15 to 20 minutes. It's ready when large bubbles break across the whole surface and don't settle when stirred."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Steep the sorrel overnight: Turn off the heat and move the pot off the burner. Stir in the dried sorrel until it is submerged. Cover and let it steep on the stovetop for 12 to 18 hours, until the liquid is deep, dark red and smells strongly of cloves and cinnamon. (You can add the sugar at this stage instead of in step 3.)"
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Strain and sweeten: Pour through a fine-mesh strainer into a large bowl or pitcher. Stop before the last bit of liquid, which holds gritty sediment, and discard the petals and spices. Stir in the sugar until no granules remain. For raw brown sugar, warm 2 cups of the strained sorrel in a small saucepan over medium heat, stir in the sugar until dissolved, then pour it back into the rest. Stir in rum or vodka to taste, if using."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Bottle, rest and chill: Pour into clean bottles and let sit for a few hours so the flavors settle. Refrigerate until cold and serve over ice. If it's too strong, add water a little at a time, keeping in mind that melting ice dilutes it further."
      }
    ],
    "yields": 12,
    "prepTime": "PT1H",
    "cookTime": "PT20M",
    "totalTime": "PT13H20M",
