    "description": "Oxtail is one of those dishes that scream comfort food. Once you sink your teeth and wrap your lips around a soft, fall of the bone, juicy, succulent, flavorful piece of oxtail there is no going back.",
    "instructions": [
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Clean the oxtail. Trim the excess fat. Wash the oxtail with the juice of a lime or lemon, or with several changes of water, rubbing it between your fingers. Drain."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Season. Season the oxtail with the green seasoning, onion, tomato, pimento peppers, hot pepper, thyme, salt and black pepper. Marinate for up to 24 hours in the refrigerator. Bring it back to room temperature before cooking."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Burn the sugar. Heat the oil in a Dutch oven or heavy-bottomed pot over medium heat. Add the brown sugar and let it bubble, froth, expand and darken, about 5 to 6 minutes. When it is almost black and close to smoking, add the ketchup and cook for a few seconds."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Sear. Add the seasoned oxtail with any liquid from the bowl. Raise the heat to high and stir constantly to coat and sear the meat, about 5 minutes."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Cook it in its own juices. Cover, reduce the heat to low and let the oxtail cook and release its juices until the liquid evaporates, about 1 hour. Stir and check every 15 minutes. Once the liquid has evaporated, stir for 2 more minutes to develop the flavor."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Add the vegetables. Stir in the bell pepper, carrots, celery and allspice and cook for 1 minute."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Simmer until tender. Add enough water to just cover the oxtail, bring it to a boil, then cover, lower the heat and simmer for 1 to 2 hours, until the oxtail is falling-apart tender and the sauce has thickened. Keep adding water as needed. Add the potatoes during the last hour, if using."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Add the butter beans. When the oxtail is almost done, stir in the butter beans (if using) and a little water for extra sauce. Cover and cook on low for 10 to 15 minutes. Taste and add more salt and pepper if needed."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Serve. Remove the thyme sprigs and pimento seeds and serve hot."
      }
    ],
