    "description": "Dried channa is soaked overnight and pressure-cooked in the Instant Pot, then seasoned, bounjayed in curry and finished on the stovetop with potatoes.",
    "instructions": [
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Soak and pressure-cook the channa (High Pressure): Rinse the dried chickpeas and soak them overnight in plenty of water. Drain and rinse. Add the channa to the Instant Pot with 6 cups fresh water and pressure-cook on High for 15 minutes. Natural or quick release both work; use quick release if you do not want the chickpeas too soft. Drain the channa, reserving the cooking liquid."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Season the cooked channa (no heat): In a large bowl, combine the pressure-cooked channa with the green seasoning, Himalayan salt, sliced onion, pimento peppers, hot pepper, garlic and scallions. Mix well and set aside."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Cook the aromatics (medium heat): Heat the extra virgin olive oil in a large heavy-bottomed pot over medium heat. Add the 1/2 sliced onion and hot pepper and cook for 3 to 5 minutes, until fragrant and the edges of the onion begin to brown."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Cook the curry (medium heat): Add the curry powder and turmeric with a splash of water. Stir well and cook for about 5 minutes, stirring often, until the water evaporates and the curry begins separating from the oil."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Bounjay the channa (medium heat, uncovered): Add the seasoned channa and stir to coat it in the curry. Cook uncovered for about 5 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the excess moisture reduces and the channa starts absorbing the curry and seasonings."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Add the potatoes and liquid: Stir in the potatoes and thyme. Pour in the reserved channa liquid and enough hot water to just cover the channa and potatoes, about 4 cups of liquid in total, depending on the width of your pot."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Simmer (high, then low heat): Turn the heat to high and bring to a boil. Reduce to low, cover and simmer for about 30 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the potatoes are fork-tender and the curry has thickened. Add a little hot water if it gets too thick before the potatoes are tender."
      },
      {
        "@type": "HowToStep",
        "text": "Finish (low heat): Break up a few pieces of potato with the back of your spoon and stir them into the sauce to thicken it. Stir in the roasted cumin and the culantro or cilantro. Taste and adjust the salt. Take the pot off the heat and let it rest for a few minutes before serving."
      }
    ],
